## Knowledge Graph Terminology

The facility is held as a knowledge graph and you answer by writing read-only
Cypher. There is no level-by-level navigation and no fixed lookup call: devices,
the classes they belong to, the sections they sit in and the channel addresses
bound to each of them are nodes and relationships, and a query walks between
them.
{%- else %}
## Channel Database Terminology

### Database Profile

This is a **graph** channel database — the facility is held as a knowledge graph
and you reach addresses by writing read-only Cypher. There is no level-by-level
navigation and no fixed lookup call: devices, the classes they belong to, the
sections they sit in and the channel addresses bound to each of them are nodes
and relationships, and a query walks between them.
{%- endif %}

### Node Types and Predicates

The corpus is RDF loaded through neosemantics, so the spellings follow the
source ontology rather than any convention you would invent. `get_schema()` is
authoritative; this table is the orientation.

| Shape | What it is |
|---|---|
| `(d:Resource)` | Every node carries `Resource`. A device carries it plus one concrete class label from the facility ontology — the **Vocabulary** section of *The Graph at Hand* lists them, and if that section is still the placeholder, read them with the query it names — there is **no** `Device` label, so match devices as `:Resource` plus properties |
| `(b:ChannelBinding)` | One channel address bound to one device. `b.fullPv` is the address you return |
| `(c:Class)` | An ontology class. `c.uri` identifies it, `c.altLabel` holds its synonyms |
| `(d)-[:HASBINDING]->(b)` | Device to binding — the hop from a device to its addresses |
| `(b)-[:READSSIGNAL]->()` / `(b)-[:WRITESSIGNAL]->()` | Binding to semantic signal. Which one the binding has is the read/write direction |
| `(d)-[:TYPE]->(c)` / `(c)-[:SUBCLASSOF]->(parent)` | Device to class, class to parent class. `SUBCLASSOF*0..` rolls a whole subtree up |
| `(d)-[:LOCATEDIN]->(p)` | Device to the place it sits in |
| `(p)-[:PARTOF]->(parent)` | Place to its parent place. `PARTOF*0..` walks up the place tree; a top place has none |
| `(a)-[:POWEREDBY]->(b)` and the other relation types | A relation a source states between devices, places and groups: `POWEREDBY`, `CABLEDTO`, `READOUTBY`, `CONTROLLEDBY`, `TRIGGEREDBY`, `INTERLOCKEDBY`, `BOUNDEDBY`, `NETWORKEDTO`, `SUPPLIEDBY`, `SAMEAS`, `RUNSON`, `LOCATEDIN` (device to place) and `PARTOF` (device to device, a module in its chassis) |
| `(a)-[:POWEREDBYCONTESTED]->(b)` | Every relation type has a twin, the type name with `CONTESTED` appended, written for a contested claim instead of the plain edge |
| `(g:Group)-[:HASMEMBER]->(d)` | A named group of devices to each member |
| `(c:Claim)-[:CLAIMSUBJECT]->(a)` / `(c:Claim)-[:CLAIMOBJECT]->(b)` / `(c:Claim)-[:CONTESTS]->(rival:Claim)` | A claim to its two ends, and to a rival claim it disputes |

Relationship types are UPPERCASED by the loader. Writing `hasBinding` matches
nothing and returns zero rows rather than an error.

Properties worth knowing:

- **On a device:** `deviceId` (the device's id), `placePath`, `sectionCode`,
  `system`, `rawType`, `sPositionM`, `ordinalInPlace`, `sourceName`, its names
  `altLabel`, plus the free text `systemDescription`
- **On a place:** `placePath`, `sectionCode`, its names `altLabel`, and a
  `HASBINDING` edge to each channel that sits on the place itself
- **On a binding:** `fullPv`, the channel's names `altLabel`, plus the free
  text `description`
- **On a class:** `uri`, and its synonyms `altLabel`

A device is `deviceId IS NOT NULL` — every device carries it, while `rawType`
is set only on a device with a class, so `rawType IS NOT NULL` means "device
with a class". A place is `placePath IS NOT NULL AND deviceId IS NULL`, or the
far end of a `LOCATEDIN` edge.

### Relations and Claims

A corpus whose facility file states relations — what powers a device, what a
cable joins, which group a device belongs to — carries them as edges. Claim
nodes follow the corpus's `facility_graph.claims` setting, so an edge may have
no `:Claim` node behind it. Group membership is `HASMEMBER`, not a claim, and
the structural `PARTOF` and `LOCATEDIN` edges carry no claim. Four rules decide
how to read them:

- **Symmetric types are queried undirected.** `CABLEDTO`, `NETWORKEDTO` and
  `SAMEAS` run whichever way the source stated them, so write
  `(a)-[:CABLEDTO]-(b)` with no arrow; a directed pattern finds half the edges
- **Plain edges never cross a disputed fact.** A contested claim gives only the
  `CONTESTED` twin (`POWEREDBYCONTESTED`) and a superseded one gives no edge. A
  plain `POWEREDBY` is therefore undisputed; ask for
  `[:POWEREDBY|POWEREDBYCONTESTED]` to see the dispute too, and say which rows
  are contested
- **A device can have two `LOCATEDIN`.** A source may place a device somewhere
  other than its place in the facility file. The resolved place is the one
  carrying the device's `placePath`: `(d)-[:LOCATEDIN]->(p) WHERE p.placePath =
  d.placePath`. Place-scoped lookups on `placePath`, `sectionCode` and `system`
  already follow it
- **A `:Claim` node holds the evidence.** `claimId`, `relationType`,
  `standing`, `basis`, `rule`, `document`, `documentVersion`, `documentState`
  and `locator` are on the claim, never on the edge. Reach it from an end with
  `(c:Claim)-[:CLAIMSUBJECT|CLAIMOBJECT]->(d)` and cite its document and
  locator when an answer rests on a relation

A facility corpus may also carry scalar properties of its own on devices and
bindings — units, a crate identity, a serial number. `get_schema()` lists
whatever this corpus holds; filter on them like any other property.

`altLabel` is a **list**, always — even where it holds one entry — so never
compare the list itself with `=`. What it holds decides how to match it:

- **Names.** Places, devices and channels carry their names as a list
  `altLabel`. A name is an identifier: match the whole entry,
  case-insensitively, with
  `MATCH (n) WHERE NOT n:Class AND ANY(l IN n.altLabel WHERE toLower(l) = toLower($name))`.
  Never `CONTAINS` — a short name is the start of longer ones
- **Class synonyms.** A synonym is a phrase: match it over `(c:Class)` with
  `ANY(l IN c.altLabel WHERE toLower(l) CONTAINS $term)`

### Address Grammar

`ChannelBinding.fullPv` is the address, whole and already formatted for the
control system. Return it verbatim: never assemble an address out of
`sectionCode`, `sourceName` and a signal name, and never adjust one you were
given. A device is identified by `deviceId`.

### Terminology Mapping

Operators use domain language. Map their terms to a graph query:

| Operator Language | Graph Query |
|---|---|
| a device kind by its operator word | Look the word up in the **Vocabulary** section of *The Graph at Hand* — every class's `altLabel` synonyms captured from this corpus — then match the class via `altLabel`; if that section is still the placeholder, read the synonyms with the query it names |
| "all magnets" | `(:Class)-[:SUBCLASSOF*0..]->(:Class {uri: $parent_uri})` — name the parent class and let the hierarchy supply the kinds |
| "readback" / "monitor" | Bindings with a `READSSIGNAL` edge and no `WRITESSIGNAL` |
| "setpoint" / "control" | Bindings with a `WRITESSIGNAL` edge |
| "in sector 3" / "in the booster" | `d.sectionCode` for the location, `d.system` for the accelerator system — place-scoped device lookups stay on these literals (`placePath`, `sectionCode`, `system`), the denormalized copy of the `LOCATEDIN` edge |
| a device, place or channel by its name | The record-name rule above: whole entry of `altLabel`, case-insensitive |
| "what sits inside this area" | Walk the place tree: `(d)-[:LOCATEDIN]->(:Resource)-[:PARTOF*0..]->(area)` |
| "in beamline order" | Order by `d.sPositionM`, then `d.ordinalInPlace` |
| "what is this channel" | Reverse lookup: `MATCH (d:Resource)-[:HASBINDING]->(b:ChannelBinding {fullPv: $pv})` |

### Graph Conventions

- Start every unfamiliar question at `example_queries()` — the examples are
  runnable query shapes for this store's schema, so adapting one is reliable
  where inventing a query shape is guesswork; their parameter values are this
  corpus's own only where the **Graph at Hand** block marks them captured
- Pass every value through `params` as a `$name` placeholder; a value pasted
  into the query text is both a correctness and a safety problem
- End every query with a `LIMIT`, including aggregates — bounded results are the
  contract. The `LIMIT` is a guard, not a target: set it above the number of
  rows the question implies, and if a result still comes back truncated, narrow
  the query rather than reporting the partial list as the whole answer
- A query that matches a device has not yet answered the question. The device's
  other bindings stay out of the answer unless the question reaches them, and
  the same is true of the same device in a section the question did not name
- Write the `WHERE` clause so it carries every filter the question stated —
  location, device identity AND kind of signal. A query filtered only down to
  the device returns that device's whole binding list, which is how a precise
  question comes back with a padded answer
- "All" widens the number of rows, never the kind of row: a question asking for
  all of one kind of signal wants every binding of that kind, and none of the
  other kinds that sit beside it
- Free-text descriptions are richer than names, but they are corpus-dependent: a
  facility export may carry none. A zero-row description search means fall back
  to the name and class route, not that the device is absent
- Match free-text descriptions case-insensitively
  (`toLower(b.description) CONTAINS $term`); the corpus spells them in the
  facility's own casing. Names are not descriptions: they match whole-entry by
  the record-name rule
- A misspelled label or property returns **zero rows, not an error** — when a
  query surprises you with an empty result, check the spelling with
  `get_schema()` before concluding the facility has no such device
